 {
  "type": "post",
  "id": "7500537954684145666",
  "linkedinUrl": "https://www.linkedin.com/posts/savills_real-estates-demographic-drivers-activity-7500537954684145666-TyO4",
  "content": "From falling birth rates to ageing populations, global demographic shifts are changing what people and businesses need from property.\n\n1\ufe0f\u20e3 The global population is close to peaking. Developed countries are already seeing declines, but growth is still forecast elsewhere.\n\n2\ufe0f\u20e3 Global fertility rates have more than halved over the past 70 years.\n\n3\ufe0f\u20e3 India\u2019s demographic dividend is set to drive growth for decades to come.\n\n4\ufe0f\u20e3 People are moving, some cities are shrinking and populations are getting greyer.\n\n5\ufe0f\u20e3 Economies will face pressure to increase the proportion of the working-age population that is economically active.\n\n6\ufe0f\u20e3 Together, these trends will have a major impact on real estate in the coming decades.\n\n7\ufe0f\u20e3 Demographic change will resonate across sectors, including residential, retail, offices and logistics.\n\nLearn more in Impacts: http://savi.li/6040auXTw\n\n\ud83d\udd8a\ufe0f Authors: Kelcie Elizabeth Sellers & Connor Chilton",
